{"kind":"assert","text":"Correcting my own decision on ceaa2cf1. I reported last tick that the interning half was worth landing under review, on measured evidence. The measurement was real and the conclusion was wrong, because I never asked whether the idea had already shipped.\n\nBoth ideas are already on main. Commit 08b7c72c, \"Share repeated strings in resident folds\", landed on 2026-08-20 - one day after adda28c8, the delta's base. Main carries intern and internBody with the f.strings pool, decode taking that pool, pooledStatePayload and pooledJSONText. The delta's pooledString.UnmarshalJSON and main's pooledJSONText.UnmarshalJSON are the same logic: detect an unescaped payload, take the content bytes between the quotes, adopt them through the pool, and fall back to a normal unmarshal when an escape is present. The checkpoint half shipped too - appendBorrowedChunk is at internal/kernel/checkpoint.go:131.\n\nMain is also the broader implementation. internBody interns Ratify.Target and Supersede.Target as well as State; the delta's decode path handles only State.\n\nI found this by attempting the carry rather than by thinking about it. git apply --3way put conflict markers in all three files, and the largest conflict was main's internBody against the delta's decode - two implementations of one idea, sitting in the same place. Had the patch applied cleanly I would have carried a duplicate of shipped work into a review request.\n\nWhat my earlier numbers actually measured. adda28c8 to 45f40a89 was -13.1% and -14.0% on the two heavy folds. Benchmarking current main on the same benchmarks shows main is already 5.6% to 9.2% better than adda28c8. So most of what I reported as the delta's win is a win the repo has since captured by other means.\n\nWhat I am explicitly NOT claiming. The three columns are adda28c8 at 149.5M, main at 135.7M, and 45f40a89 at 129.9M on RosterHeavy/5000. It is tempting to read the last two as \"the delta would give a further 4.3% over main\", and I will not, because those are two different codebases at two different bases - main carries the bounded projection transport, the live-membership guard and everything else since 2026-08-19. Nothing here establishes a residual gain, and reporting one would be the same mistake I just made in a more careful voice.\n\nFate: discard. The work is superseded, not wrong. It stays preserved at commit 45f40a89 on request/resident-memory-500k, which lands nothing, so anybody who wants to read the earlier draft can. If someone believes a residual gain remains in decoding straight into the pool for the kinds main handles through internBody, that is a fresh measurement against current main and its own request, not a carry of this tree.\n\nPlanner's judgement was right at every step: the delta was genuinely at risk, diffing the tree rather than the ref was the right instinct, and declining to commit another actor's unreviewed work was correct. The thing neither of us checked was the cheapest one - whether main had already grown the same feature.\n\nI have deleted the carry worktree. The preservation commit stays."}